vpss/scaler: export sclr_disp_frame_start_poll()

soph_drm owns the DISP but not the shared VIP interrupt line (cv181x_vpss
does), so it cannot take the DISP frame interrupts and has no way to know
when the display crosses a frame boundary.

A shadowed DISP register written with the shadow mask clear reaches the
working bank by itself at the next frame start; a force_up applies it at
once, mid-scan. So a page flip needs no trigger, only the knowledge of when
the frame start that latched it has happened, which is when the old buffer
stops being read.

Export a poll of the raw DISP frame-start status (INTR_STATUS bit 0, cleared
write-1-to-clear). It latches whether or not the interrupt is wanted, and
nothing else on this board clears it. Frame-end (bit 1) is no use for this:
the VIP interrupt handler clears it within microseconds.

Signed-off-by: badgeos
---
--- a/interdrv/vpss/chip/cv181x/scaler.c
+++ b/interdrv/vpss/chip/cv181x/scaler.c
@@ -1452,6 +1452,26 @@
 }
 
 /**
+ * sclr_disp_frame_start_poll - has the DISP started a frame since last poll?
+ *
+ * Reads the raw DISP frame-start status (INTR_STATUS bit 0) and clears it
+ * write-1-to-clear. A shadowed DISP register written before a frame start is
+ * live after it, so a driver that owns the DISP but not the VIP interrupt line
+ * can poll this to learn when a page flip has landed.
+ * Returns true exactly on the polls that observe a frame start.
+ */
+bool sclr_disp_frame_start_poll(void)
+{
+	u32 st = _reg_read(reg_base + REG_SCL_TOP_INTR_STATUS);
+
+	if (!(st & BIT(0)))
+		return false;
+	_reg_write(reg_base + REG_SCL_TOP_INTR_STATUS, BIT(0));
+	return true;
+}
+EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(sclr_disp_frame_start_poll);
+
+/**
  * sclr_intr_status - sclr's interrupt status
  *                    check 'union sclr_intr' for each bit mask
  *
